---
---
<section id="faq" class="relative isolate overflow-hidden py-12 sm:py-16 lg:py-24 bg-sky-50/70">
  <div class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 bg-[radial-gradient(circle_at_16%_18%,rgba(14,165,233,0.16),transparent_34%),radial-gradient(circle_at_84%_14%,rgba(15,23,42,0.08),transparent_28%),linear-gradient(to_bottom,rgba(255,255,255,0.84),rgba(241,245,249,0.92))]"></div>
  <svg class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-10 h-full w-full stroke-slate-900/6 mask-[linear-gradient(to_bottom,white,transparent)]" aria-hidden="true">
    <defs>
      <pattern id="faq-grid" width="72" height="72" x="50%" y="-1" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
        <path d="M.5 72V.5H72" fill="none" />
      </pattern>
    </defs>
    <rect width="100%" height="100%" stroke-width="0" fill="url(#faq-grid)" />
  </svg>

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-7xl px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="mb-12 space-y-4 sm:mb-16 lg:mb-24">
      <div class="text-sky-800 text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-widest">FAQ</div>
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold md:text-4xl text-slate-900 tracking-tight">Questions We Hear Often</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg text-slate-600 font-light leading-relaxed">
        Appeals move quickly and the rules are technical. These answers cover a few of the most common questions clients ask when they are deciding what to do next.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl">
      <div class="mb-6 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white/85 p-5 shadow-sm backdrop-blur-sm sm:p-6">
        <div class="inline-flex rounded-full border border-sky-200 bg-sky-50 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-sky-800">
          Strict deadlines. Careful review.
        </div>
        <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-base sm:text-lg font-light leading-relaxed text-slate-600">
          If you are unsure whether you have a viable appeal, the safest next step is a focused review of the judgment and the trial record.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="group overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-sm transition-all duration-200 open:shadow-md">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-5 text-left text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 transition-colors hover:text-sky-800 sm:px-6">
            <span>Can I appeal just because I am unhappy with the judge's decision?</span>
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="size-5 shrink-0 text-slate-500 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path></svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-5 pt-0 text-base font-light leading-relaxed text-slate-600 sm:px-6">
            No. An appeal is not a "do-over" or a second chance to present new evidence. To win an appeal, we must prove that the trial judge made a specific legal error or abused their discretion in a way that significantly impacted the outcome of your case.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-sm transition-all duration-200 open:shadow-md">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-5 text-left text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 transition-colors hover:text-sky-800 sm:px-6">
            <span>How long do I have to file an appeal?</span>
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="size-5 shrink-0 text-slate-500 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path></svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-5 pt-0 text-base font-light leading-relaxed text-slate-600 sm:px-6">
            The window to file a Notice of Appeal is strictly limited, often 30 days or less from the date the final judgment is entered, depending on your jurisdiction. If you miss this deadline, you generally lose your right to appeal forever. It is critical to consult an appellate attorney immediately after a ruling.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-sm transition-all duration-200 open:shadow-md">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-5 text-left text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 transition-colors hover:text-sky-800 sm:px-6">
            <span>My ex-spouse is appealing a judgment I won. Do I need an appellate attorney?</span>
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="size-5 shrink-0 text-slate-500 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path></svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-5 pt-0 text-base font-light leading-relaxed text-slate-600 sm:px-6">
            Yes. Defending a judgment on appeal requires the same specialized legal research and brief-writing skills as filing one. You need an advocate who understands appellate procedure to dismantle the appellant's arguments and protect your hard-won victory.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="group overflow-hidden rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white shadow-sm transition-all duration-200 open:shadow-md">
          <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-5 text-left text-lg font-semibold text-slate-900 transition-colors hover:text-sky-800 sm:px-6">
            <span>Why shouldn't I just use my trial lawyer for the appeal?</span>
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" class="size-5 shrink-0 text-slate-500 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-180"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"></path></svg>
          </summary>
          <div class="px-5 pb-5 pt-0 text-base font-light leading-relaxed text-slate-600 sm:px-6">
            Trial attorneys excel at presenting evidence, questioning witnesses, and arguing before a judge. Appellate attorneys specialize in deep legal research, identifying procedural flaws, and writing highly technical briefs for a panel of appellate judges. A fresh, objective perspective is often exactly what is needed to spot errors the trial attorney might have missed.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>